---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="Local Traditions & Culture" description="Aymara heritage, Afro-Bolivian culture, ayllu governance, festivals, coca traditions, and language." heroImage="/images/yungas/tocana-landscape.jpg">

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="relative min-h-[40vh] flex items-end pb-12 overflow-hidden">
    <img src="/images/yungas/tocana-landscape.jpg" alt="Tocana village landscape, Afro-Bolivian community in the Yungas" width="1920" height="1440" class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full object-cover" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" />
    <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-black/80 via-black/40 to-black/20"></div>
    <div class="relative z-10 max-w-[1400px] mx-auto px-6 lg:px-10 w-full">
      <span class="text-label text-gold">Culture</span>
      <h1 class="text-headline text-white mt-2">Local Traditions & Culture</h1>
      <p class="text-body-lg text-bark-200 mt-2 max-w-xl">
        Understanding and respecting the communities that have been here for centuries
        is not optional &mdash; it's foundational.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Introduction -->
  <section class="py-20 px-6 lg:px-10">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1.1rem] leading-[1.85] mb-6 reveal">
        BARK is being built in a place with deep roots. The Yungas has been home to Aymara
        communities for millennia, to Afro-Bolivian communities for centuries, and to
        agricultural cooperatives that predate any foreign project. Understanding these
        traditions is not a cultural nicety &mdash; it is a prerequisite for building anything
        meaningful here.
      </p>
      <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1.1rem] leading-[1.85] reveal" data-delay="100">
        This page exists because BARK's credibility depends on demonstrating genuine respect
        for the place we are joining. These traditions are not tourist attractions. They are
        living cultures with governance systems, economic models, and spiritual practices
        that have sustained communities through conditions far harsher than what we will face.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Aymara Heritage -->
  <section class="bg-bark-100 py-24 px-6 lg:px-10">
    <div class="max-w-[1400px] mx-auto">
      <h2 class="text-title text-bark-800 mb-3 reveal">Aymara Heritage</h2>
      <p class="text-bark-500 mb-10 max-w-2xl reveal" data-delay="100">
        The Yungas population is predominantly Aymara indigenous. Their traditions are
        not historical artifacts &mdash; they are active, practiced, and protected by
        Bolivia's 2009 Constitution.
      </p>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
        <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-xl border border-bark-200 p-10 reveal" data-delay="200">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg mb-4">Language & Identity</h3>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Aymara is widely spoken in the Yungas, especially in rural areas. Bolivia's Constitution
            recognizes 36 official languages, and Aymara is one of the most widely spoken alongside
            Quechua and Spanish. In many communities, Aymara is the first language and Spanish is
            learned for commerce and government interaction.
          </p>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
            BARK community members should learn basic Aymara. Not because it's required for survival,
            but because it signals respect. Speaking even a few words in someone's language changes
            the relationship entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-xl border border-bark-200 p-10 reveal" data-delay="300">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg mb-4">Agricultural Traditions</h3>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Aymara agricultural knowledge represents thousands of years of adaptation to Andean terrain.
            Terracing, seasonal planting cycles tied to astronomical observation, seed selection,
            and water management &mdash; these are not primitive techniques. They are highly refined
            systems that sustained large populations before European contact.
          </p>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
            BARK's permaculture and regenerative agriculture programs should integrate local knowledge,
            not replace it. The Aymara know this land. We are the newcomers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-xl border border-bark-200 p-10 reveal" data-delay="400">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg mb-4">Spiritual Practices</h3>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Aymara spiritual life is connected to <strong class="text-bark-800">Pachamama</strong>
            (earth mother) and <strong class="text-bark-800">Achachilas</strong> (mountain spirits).
            These are not metaphors &mdash; they are experienced as real relationships with the living world.
            Offerings to Pachamama before planting, building, or beginning any significant endeavor
            are standard practice.
          </p>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
            BARK should participate in these practices when invited, not as performance but as genuine
            engagement with a worldview that honors the land we share.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-xl border border-bark-200 p-10 reveal" data-delay="500">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg mb-4">Community Labor Systems</h3>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Two systems govern mutual aid in Aymara communities:
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-3">
            <li class="text-bark-600 text-sm pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
              <strong class="text-bark-800">Ayni</strong> &mdash; Reciprocal labor exchange. I help you harvest,
              you help me build. The debt is personal and specific. This is the foundation of
              Andean economics.
            </li>
            <li class="text-bark-600 text-sm pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
              <strong class="text-bark-800">Minka</strong> &mdash; Communal labor for community projects.
              Everyone works on shared infrastructure &mdash; roads, irrigation, public buildings.
              The benefit is collective, not individual.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mt-4">
            Bolivia has 15,000 active cooperatives. The cooperative tradition is not imported &mdash;
            it is rooted in these ancestral practices.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Afro-Bolivian Communities -->
  <section class="py-24 px-6 lg:px-10">
    <div class="max-w-[1400px] mx-auto">
      <h2 class="text-title text-bark-800 mb-3 reveal">Afro-Bolivian Communities</h2>
      <p class="text-bark-500 mb-10 max-w-2xl reveal" data-delay="100">
        Tocana, Mururata, and Chijchipa are Afro-Bolivian communities in Nor Yungas &mdash;
        among the closest neighbors to any BARK site. This relationship is not optional.
        It is central.
      </p>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-8 mb-12">
        <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-xl border border-bark-200 p-8 reveal" data-delay="200">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 mb-3">Tocana</h3>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-3">
            Birthplace of the <strong class="text-bark-800">saya</strong> rhythm &mdash; a traditional
            Afro-Bolivian musical genre with drums and chants. Cultural heart of the Afro-Bolivian
            community and a center for cultural tourism from Coroico.
          </p>
          <p class="text-bark-500 text-xs">Primary crops: coca, coffee, citrus, cacao, bananas</p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-xl border border-bark-200 p-8 reveal" data-delay="300">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 mb-3">Mururata</h3>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-3">
            Home of <strong class="text-bark-800">Julio Pinedo</strong>, the symbolic king of the
            Afro-Bolivians &mdash; a ceremonial monarchy recognized by the Bolivian state. Bolivia
            recognized its first Afro-Bolivian king in the Yungas, a significant act of cultural
            acknowledgment.
          </p>
          <p class="text-bark-500 text-xs">Part of the Nor Yungas Afro-Bolivian corridor</p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-xl border border-bark-200 p-8 reveal" data-delay="400">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 mb-3">Heritage & History</h3>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-3">
            Ancestors were forcibly brought from Africa to work coca, coffee, and sugar cane
            haciendas. Despite centuries of marginalization, these communities maintain rich
            cultural heritage. Approximately 15,000&ndash;25,000 Afro-Bolivians nationally,
            with a major concentration in the Nor and Sud Yungas.
          </p>
          <p class="text-bark-500 text-xs">~0.2% of Bolivia's population; ongoing struggle for recognition</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-bark-100 rounded-xl p-8 border border-bark-200 reveal" data-delay="500">
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 mb-3">Saya: Living Musical Tradition</h3>
        <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Saya is not a museum piece. It is a vibrant, living musical and dance tradition performed
          at festivals, celebrations, and community gatherings throughout the Yungas. The drums,
          chants, and choreography carry the memory of African heritage adapted to Andean life over
          centuries. In 2025, Afro-Bolivians continue to "fight invisibility with dance and memory."
        </p>
        <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-bark-800">For BARK:</strong> Building genuine relationships with Afro-Bolivian
          communities means learning about saya, attending performances when invited, and supporting
          cultural preservation &mdash; not as patrons, but as neighbors. These communities have deep
          local agricultural knowledge (they've been farming the Yungas for centuries) that is directly
          relevant to BARK's food production goals.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Ayllu Governance -->
  <section class="bg-bark-100 bg-warm-gradient py-24 px-6 lg:px-10">
    <div class="max-w-[1400px] mx-auto">
      <h2 class="text-title text-bark-800 mb-3 reveal">Ayllu Governance</h2>
      <p class="text-bark-500 mb-10 max-w-2xl reveal" data-delay="100">
        The ayllu is the traditional Andean community governance unit, predating the Inca Empire.
        It is not a historical curiosity &mdash; Bolivia's 2009 Constitution formally recognizes
        ayllu governance, and it continues to function in many communities.
      </p>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-px bg-bark-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden reveal" data-delay="200">
        <div class="bg-bark-50 p-10">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg mb-4">How It Works</h3>
          <ul class="space-y-3">
            <li class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
              Extended kinship-based communities with collective land management
            </li>
            <li class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
              Decisions made in community assemblies &mdash; not by appointed leaders
            </li>
            <li class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
              <strong class="text-bark-800">Rotating authority</strong> &mdash; leadership positions rotate,
              preventing permanent power concentration
            </li>
            <li class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
              Consensus decision-making in community assemblies
            </li>
            <li class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
              Recognized as "territorial grassroots organisations" under Bolivian law
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-bark-50 p-10">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-lg mb-4">Why It Matters for BARK</h3>
          <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mb-4">
            BARK's governance design should draw from the ayllu model, not just Western cooperative
            frameworks. The ayllu offers:
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-3">
            <li class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
              Proven model for collective land management (centuries of evidence)
            </li>
            <li class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
              Anti-corruption through rotating leadership (no permanent power)
            </li>
            <li class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
              Constitutional recognition in the country where BARK operates
            </li>
            <li class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
              Cultural legitimacy with local communities who practice it
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-8 bg-bark-50 rounded-xl p-8 border border-bark-200 reveal" data-delay="300">
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 mb-3">The Documented Reality</h3>
        <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mb-3">
          Academic research from Northern Potosi documents how even well-meaning progressive organizations
          and unions have sometimes marginalized the democratic internal life of ayllus while claiming
          to support indigenous governance. A case study from Bolivar Province shows how overlapping
          authorities &mdash; peasant union, ayllu, municipal council &mdash; create tensions that communities
          navigate pragmatically.
        </p>
        <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-bark-800">Lesson for BARK:</strong> Respecting ayllu governance means not assuming
          BARK's governance model is superior. It means listening, learning, and integrating local wisdom
          into our structures &mdash; not imposing imported frameworks that may be less tested than what already exists.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-8 bg-bark-50 rounded-xl p-8 border border-bark-200 reveal" data-delay="400">
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 mb-3">Sindicatos Agrarios in the Yungas</h3>
        <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mb-3">
          In the Yungas specifically, daily governance often operates through
          <strong class="text-bark-800">sindicatos agrarios</strong> (agricultural unions) rather than
          the ayllu system, which is more prevalent on the altiplano. These sindicatos organize land use,
          resolve disputes, and coordinate community labor. They are the practical authority that shapes
          everyday life in most Yungas communities.
        </p>
        <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-bark-800">For BARK:</strong> Any project in the Yungas must engage with the
          local sindicato, not just the municipal government. Ignoring the sindicato is ignoring the
          actual governance structure that people rely on.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Festivals -->
  <section class="py-24 px-6 lg:px-10">
    <div class="max-w-[1400px] mx-auto">
      <h2 class="text-title text-bark-800 mb-3 reveal">Festivals & Calendar</h2>
      <p class="text-bark-500 mb-10 max-w-2xl reveal" data-delay="100">
        The Yungas calendar is rich with celebrations that blend indigenous, colonial, and
        Afro-Bolivian traditions. BARK should participate in local festivals, not create
        parallel events.
      </p>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-px bg-bark-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden reveal" data-delay="200">
        <div class="bg-bark-50 p-8">
          <span class="text-label text-gold text-[0.6rem]">February/March</span>
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-sm mt-2 mb-3">Carnival</h3>
          <p class="text-bark-500 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Major celebration in Coroico. Water balloons, music, dancing, parades.
            One of the most vibrant events in the Yungas calendar. The entire town participates.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-bark-50 p-8">
          <span class="text-label text-gold text-[0.6rem]">May</span>
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-sm mt-2 mb-3">Fiesta de la Cruz</h3>
          <p class="text-bark-500 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Festival of the Cross. A syncretic celebration blending Catholic and indigenous traditions.
            Community gatherings, processions, music, and shared meals mark the occasion.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-bark-50 p-8">
          <span class="text-label text-gold text-[0.6rem]">Seasonal</span>
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-sm mt-2 mb-3">Agricultural Festivals</h3>
          <p class="text-bark-500 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Tied to planting and harvest cycles. These are not scheduled by calendar dates
            but by agricultural readiness. Community labor (minka) often coincides with these events.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-bark-50 p-8">
          <span class="text-label text-gold text-[0.6rem]">November</span>
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 text-sm mt-2 mb-3">Todos Santos</h3>
          <p class="text-bark-500 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            All Saints' Day. Families honor deceased relatives with elaborate offerings, food,
            and visits to cemeteries. One of the most culturally significant dates in Bolivian life.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Coca Tradition -->
  <section class="bg-bark-100 py-24 px-6 lg:px-10">
    <div class="max-w-[1400px] mx-auto">
      <div class="max-w-3xl bar-section">
        <h2 class="text-title text-bark-800 mb-6 reveal">Coca Tradition</h2>
        <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1.1rem] leading-[1.85] mb-6 reveal" data-delay="100">
          Coca leaf is sacred in Andean culture. It has been used for millennia &mdash; long before
          anyone conceived of processing it into cocaine. The leaf itself is mild, nutritious,
          and deeply embedded in every aspect of Yungas life.
        </p>

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-8 reveal" data-delay="200">
          <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-xl border border-bark-200 p-8">
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 mb-3">Traditional Uses</h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2">
              <li class="text-bark-600 text-sm pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
                <strong class="text-bark-800">Acullico</strong> &mdash; chewing coca leaves for energy, altitude adaptation, and social bonding
              </li>
              <li class="text-bark-600 text-sm pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
                <strong class="text-bark-800">Divination</strong> &mdash; reading coca leaves for guidance and decision-making
              </li>
              <li class="text-bark-600 text-sm pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
                <strong class="text-bark-800">Medicine</strong> &mdash; digestive aid, pain relief, altitude sickness prevention
              </li>
              <li class="text-bark-600 text-sm pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
                <strong class="text-bark-800">Social bonding</strong> &mdash; offering coca is a gesture of friendship and respect
              </li>
              <li class="text-bark-600 text-sm pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
                <strong class="text-bark-800">Offerings</strong> &mdash; integral part of Pachamama ceremonies and rituals
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-xl border border-bark-200 p-8">
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 mb-3">Legal Status</h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2">
              <li class="text-bark-600 text-sm pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
                Legal in Bolivia, protected by the Constitution
              </li>
              <li class="text-bark-600 text-sm pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
                Yungas is one of two authorized coca-growing regions
              </li>
              <li class="text-bark-600 text-sm pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
                Yungas coca is traditional, high-quality, primarily for domestic consumption
              </li>
              <li class="text-bark-600 text-sm pl-5 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.55em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:rounded-full before:bg-accent/60">
                Bolivia successfully lobbied the UN to remove coca chewing from the international ban (2013)
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="text-bark-600 text-[1.1rem] leading-[1.85] reveal" data-delay="300">
          <strong class="text-bark-800">BARK's position is unambiguous:</strong> We accept and respect
          coca tradition completely. Disrespecting coca would mean disrespecting our neighbors,
          our host communities, and the culture of the place where we choose to build. Anyone
          who cannot accept this aspect of Yungas life should not join BARK.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Language -->
  <section class="py-24 px-6 lg:px-10">
    <div class="max-w-[1400px] mx-auto">
      <h2 class="text-title text-bark-800 mb-3 reveal">Language</h2>
      <p class="text-bark-500 mb-10 max-w-2xl reveal" data-delay="100">
        Spanish is the primary language for commerce and government, but Aymara is widely
        spoken in the Yungas. Bolivia's Constitution recognizes 36 official languages.
      </p>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
        <div class="reveal" data-delay="200">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 mb-6">Essential Aymara Phrases</h3>
          <div class="space-y-3">
            <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-lg border border-bark-200 p-4 flex items-center justify-between">
              <div>
                <span class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800">Kamisaki</span>
                <span class="text-bark-400 text-sm ml-2">(kah-mee-SAH-kee)</span>
              </div>
              <span class="text-bark-500 text-sm">Hello / How are you?</span>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-lg border border-bark-200 p-4 flex items-center justify-between">
              <div>
                <span class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800">Waliki</span>
                <span class="text-bark-400 text-sm ml-2">(wah-LEE-kee)</span>
              </div>
              <span class="text-bark-500 text-sm">Fine / Good</span>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-lg border border-bark-200 p-4 flex items-center justify-between">
              <div>
                <span class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800">Jallalla</span>
                <span class="text-bark-400 text-sm ml-2">(hah-YAH-yah)</span>
              </div>
              <span class="text-bark-500 text-sm">Cheers / Long live!</span>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-lg border border-bark-200 p-4 flex items-center justify-between">
              <div>
                <span class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800">Yuspagara</span>
                <span class="text-bark-400 text-sm ml-2">(yoos-pah-GAH-rah)</span>
              </div>
              <span class="text-bark-500 text-sm">Thank you</span>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-lg border border-bark-200 p-4 flex items-center justify-between">
              <div>
                <span class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800">Janiwa</span>
                <span class="text-bark-400 text-sm ml-2">(hah-NEE-wah)</span>
              </div>
              <span class="text-bark-500 text-sm">No</span>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-bark-50 rounded-lg border border-bark-200 p-4 flex items-center justify-between">
              <div>
                <span class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800">Jisa</span>
                <span class="text-bark-400 text-sm ml-2">(HEE-sah)</span>
              </div>
              <span class="text-bark-500 text-sm">Yes</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="reveal" data-delay="300">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 mb-6">Why Language Matters</h3>
          <div class="bg-bark-100 rounded-xl p-8 border border-bark-200">
            <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mb-4">
              Speaking basic Aymara is not about fluency &mdash; it's about intention. When a foreigner
              says "Kamisaki" instead of "Hola" to an Aymara speaker, it communicates something beyond
              the word: <em>I see you. I respect your language. I'm trying.</em>
            </p>
            <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mb-4">
              Bolivia's multilingual reality is protected by law. The 2009 Constitution requires
              all government employees to speak at least one indigenous language. This is not token
              multiculturalism &mdash; it reflects genuine political power held by indigenous communities.
            </p>
            <p class="text-bark-600 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
              BARK should offer Aymara language classes as part of community life. Spanish proficiency
              is essential for daily operations. Aymara is essential for genuine integration.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
